71875, -0.00356292724609375, 0.0182342529296875, -0.01305389404296875, -0.0071868896484375, -0.00390625, -0.0015516281127929688, -0.0280303955078125, 0.026580810546875, -0.0009441375732421875, 0.0277557373046875, 0.003177642822265625, -0.0227203369140625, -0.00374603271484375, -0.00475311279296875, -0.0109100341796875, 0.0013017654418945312, 0.027557373046875, -0.00077056884765625, 0.00952911376953125, -0.0080108642578125, 0.036376953125, -0.01296234130859375, 0.00638580322265625, -0.007396697998046875, 0.01306915283203125, -0.01428985595703125, -0.007354736328125, 0.0193634033203125, -0.043060302734375, 0.0029850006103515625, -0.0059967041015625, -0.0159454345703125, -0.0014801025390625, 0.0237274169921875, 0.0014972686767578125, -0.047119140625, 0.011749267578125, -0.0275726318359375, -0.0184783935546875, -0.003139495849609375, 0.01354217529296875, -0.01184844970703125, -0.0155029296875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Componentwise weak convergence of signed synaptic kernels alone does not determine the fast-synapse limit of a sparse threshold-reset network. Within a causal event protocol featuring clamped refractoriness and smooth positive-delay kernels, we construct two families whose excitatory and inhibitory measures both converge weakly to $\u03b4_0$, yet differ in the microscopic order of spike arrivals. In the excitatory-first family, the target neuron fires, whereas in the inhibitory-first family, it does not; this distinction occurs precisely when $x+a-b<\u03b8\\le x+a$. Strict margins ensure this response is robust to perturbations in the target state, aggregate E/I pulse masses, and bounded drift. This macroscopic effect persists on a moderately sparse, Dale-compatible random block graph with $q_N\\to\\infty$ and $q_N/N\\to0$, where both systems share the same graph and initial data. Along any deterministic joint scaling $\\varepsilon_N\\downarrow0$, their population-averaged firing counts differ by $1/2+o_{L^1}(1)$. A bounded-degree construction and subsequent probe demonstrate that this discrepancy is macroscopic and can persist through reset. Fixed positive-delay kernels with finitely many classes admit a stable regime: before grazing, typewise-mixing sparse networks converge to a delayed class mean-field system. For directed Erd\u0151s\u2013R\u00e9nyi graphs, the bound $O_P(\u03bb_N^{-1/2}+\\|\u03c0_N-\u03c0\\|_1)$ holds when $\u03bb_N\\to\\infty$ and $\u03bb_N/N\\to0$. This result separates stable averaging at fixed delay from singular collapse; in the latter, componentwise weak convergence loses the signed arrival-order information essential for the threshold-reset response."}}